{"content":[{"c":["ATHARIM"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Place, according to the ",{"c":["nlt"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},", where the Israelites sought to enter Canaan when attacked by the king of Arad (",{"href":"?bref=Num.21.1","r":"Num.21.1","rawR":"Num.21.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.21.1","value":"Num.21.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 21:1"},"). The name means “tracks” and is generally thought to be located near Tamar or Hazazon-tamar several miles south of the Dead Sea. One possible reading of the text makes it identical with Tamar. The ",{"c":["kjv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},", following the Targum and Vulgate, translates it “spies.”"],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Atharim","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Nm 21:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Num.21.1","rawQueryValue":"Num.21.1","sourceRefExact":"Num.21.1"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ed3c4ca68f8bb88a5e5debe14b435c61eb7a529a27a892c1e825194c6e6f8500","sourceItemName":"Atharim","sourceItemOrder":601,"sourceMember":"Articles/A.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Atharim","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Atharim","title":"Atharim","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}